.he "newuser (1)                   10/1/26                    newuser (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
newuser
- add a user
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
newuser
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Newuser adds one account to the system.  It takes no arguments and
asks what it needs to know.  Only the super-user may run it.
.sp
It asks for the login name first.  A name is at least two characters
long and is made of lower case letters and nothing else; anything
else is refused and asked for again.
.sp
Then it lists the groups in /etc/group and asks which the new user
belongs to.  A group is named by the same rule as a user.  If the name
is one of those listed, the user is put in it.  If it is not, a new
group of that name is made, with the lowest group number from 1 up
that no group has.
.sp
Then it asks for a comment - a real name, a phone number, an office,
whatever is wanted - which is optional, and a bare return leaves it
empty.  The comment is kept in the password file as one field, and the
field separator cannot be part of it, so any colons typed are changed
to semicolons.
.sp
The account is given the lowest user number from 11 up that
/etc/passwd has not used; the numbers below 11 are left for the
system.  Its home directory is /a/name, made with any parent that is
missing and given to the new user, and its shell is /bin/sh.  It has
no password, so passwd (1) is the next thing to run for it.
.sp
"New user added." is printed when it is done.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The password file is read in, the new line added, and the whole file
written back, so for that time nothing else may change it.  Newuser
takes a lock on it first: /etc/passwdL is made as a second name for
/etc/passwd, and a second newuser that finds it there waits a second
and tries again.  A lock more than a minute old is taken to belong to
a run that died and is broken.
.sp
Signals 1 through 14 are ignored from the time the lock is taken, so
that an interrupt cannot leave the file half written.
.sp
A name already in /etc/passwd is refused after the other questions
have been answered, and nothing is changed.  Group numbers and user
numbers run out at 255, and the message says so.
.sp
Newuser exits 0 when the account was added and 1 when it was not.  The
1982 command had the two the other way round.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/passwd - the password file
.br
/etc/passwdL - the lock on it
.br
/etc/group - the group file
.br
/a - where home directories are made
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
passwd (1), login (1), mkdir (1), owner (1), passwd (5), group (5)
